divided by the grid spacing, which corresponds to \code{"interpolated"}.

The posterior interval is pointwise: at each value it has posterior
probability \code{level} for that value, not for the whole curve at once.

\code{at}, \code{lower} and \code{upper} are specified in the units of the estimates.
\code{scale} only changes the units of the result: effect values are
multiplied by it, densities are divided by it, and probabilities are
unchanged. For example, if the estimates are differences in
proportions, \code{scale = 100} gives percentage points.
